<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Şanlıurfa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Urfa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Şanlıurfa, commonly called Urfa, is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> on the site of ancient Edessa, a centre of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and capital of the crusader County of Edessa. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was occupied by British and then French troops until a local rising and siege forced the French garrison to withdraw in April 1920. The prefix Şanlı, 'glorious', was added in 1984 in honour of that resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanliurfa.jpg" alt="Şanlıurfa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mevlid-i_Halil_Mosque_19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Urfa, officially Şanlıurfa, lies on a plain about 80 km east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, at the border between the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> and the Mesopotamian plain. The earliest name recorded for the city is Admaʾ, found in Assyrian cuneiform of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-assyrian-period">Old Assyrian period</a>. About 12 km to the north-east is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> site of Göbekli Tepe, founded in the tenth millennium BCE, and the area was part of a network of the first settlements where agriculture arose. Because of its place in Jewish, Christian and Islamic history, and a legend that it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>'s hometown, Urfa is nicknamed the City of Prophets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> founded the city as Edessa in 302 or 303 BCE, naming it after the ancient capital of Macedonia. It surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> general Iyad ibn Ghanm in 639 without resistance, and the terms guaranteed that the city's Christians would keep their cathedral. The Numayrid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waththab-ibn-sabiq">Waththab ibn Sabiq</a> annexed it after declaring independence in 990.</p><p>Urfa was the capital of the crusader County of Edessa for about half a century from 1098, ruling subjects who were a mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and Syrians, among whom the Armenians were dominant in the city itself. Nur ad-Din's nephew Sayf al-Din Ghazi II captured it after Nur ad-Din's death in 1174. The city submitted to the troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> in 1260 and its inhabitants were spared, though it was left desolate.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> gained control in the early fourteenth century and renovated the ruined citadel, but the city attracted few inhabitants because merchant traffic took another route. It was likely incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> around 1517, and an Ottoman tax register of 1518 documented 1,082 families, 700 of them Muslim and 382 Christian.</p><p>During the First World War Urfa was a site of the Armenian and Assyrian genocides from 1915. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were deported and killed; about 400 were taken to the edge of town and killed in four days from 15 to 19 August, and another massacre on 23 September killed 300.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Urfa is the capital of Şanlıurfa Province. The citadel stands on rocky heights south of the old centre, and its first known fortification dates from the early sixth century under the emperor Justinian. The old city walls date from an Abbasid rebuilding in 812. According to legend, the pool of Balıklıgöl was formed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> was thrown into a fire by Nimrod and the flames turned to water. The Şanlıurfa Museum houses some 10,000 artefacts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> through Islamic times, including finds from Göbekli Tepe, and the Haleplibahçe Mosaic Museum displays the oldest known mosaic depicting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Urfa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Ya’qub later defeated a Mamluk army near <b>Urfa</b> in 1480, and in 1488 his forces, together with those of the Shirvanshah, killed the militant Safavid leader Shaykh Haydar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ur is possibly the 'Ur of the Chaldees' that the Book of Genesis names as the birthplace of Abraham, although <b>Şanlıurfa</b>, Urkesh, Urartu and Kutha have also been proposed, and the Chaldeans did not settle nearby until about 850 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Christians of Edessa, present-day <b>Şanlıurfa</b> in Turkey, made their dialect, Syriac, the language of a great Christian tradition from the 2nd and 3rd centuries.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Sieges followed at <b>Urfa</b> and Aintab.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-cilicia">French Cilicia</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Seleucid Empire lost its eastern lands, a local dynasty of Arab origin took power at Edessa, modern <b>Şanlıurfa</b>, c. 132 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/osroene">Osroene</a></li></ul></section>
